2. Dependency Injection (DI)
What is DI?
A design pattern where dependencies are injected into classes instead of creating them manually.

Bad:

public class UserController
{
    UserService service = new UserService();
}
Good:

public class UserController
{
    private readonly IUserService _service;

    public UserController(IUserService service)
    {
        _service = service;
    }
}
Benefits
Loose Coupling
Controller doesn't know implementation details.

Easy Unit Testing
Mock<IUserService>
Better Maintainability
Service Lifetimes
Transient
New instance every time.

builder.Services.AddTransient<IUserService, UserService>();
Scoped
One instance per request.

builder.Services.AddScoped<IUserService, UserService>();
Most commonly used.

Singleton
One instance throughout application.

builder.Services.AddSingleton<IUserService, UserService>();
Senior-Level Question
What happens if Singleton uses Scoped Service?

Answer:

Lifetime mismatch issue.

ASP.NET Core throws:

Cannot consume scoped service from singleton
